  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1234 AM MDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nStill large discrepancies in the model guidance with QPF amounts\nthrough Thursday. The 18z runs of the ECMWF and GFS were vastly\ndifferent with a drier GFS and very wet ECMWF. A quick look at\nthe 00z guidance shows a downward trend with the ECMWF (although\nstill producing a lot of rainfall), and strengthening of the\nflow around the ejecting upper low.\n\nTuesday through Thursday...there is better agreement between the\n00z guidance than the previous runs. The ECMWF has trended\ntiming more in line with the GFS, but the storm track is still\nwest over Colorado and not south over the Texas Panhandle.  \n\nThe ECMWF, various AI versions of the guidance and NBM/DESI\nstill on track for heavy precipitation during this period,\nmainly along and south of Highway 50. These solutions track the\nupper low from Arizona, across New Mexico and lift it north\nacross eastern Colorado. Abundant tropical moisture embedded in\nthe upper low from remnants of Polo will wrap around the low,\nwith strong forcing and deep upslope flow for an extended period\nof time into the Raton Ridge. Winds through 500 mb are\nnortheasterly around the upper low as it ejects northward, with\nheavy precipitation forecast along the southern Sangres, east\nalong the Raton Ridge and New Mexico border, with a bulk of the\nheavy precipitation falling on Tuesday through Wednesday\nmorning. QPF forecast amounts of 1 to 3 inches, with locally\nhigher amounts are possible, with the ECMWF having the highest\ntotals. The 00z ECMWF has a quicker ejection of the upper low\ninto the Central Plains on Thursday, with precipitation\ndeparting the area by Friday morning.\n\nNow for the GFS and Canadian guidance. Both solutions track the\nupper low from southern Arizona, across New Mexico and into the\nTexas Panhandle. This guidance keeps a bulk of the precipitation\nsouth, with much stronger northerly flow across our Colorado\nPlains Wednesday and Wednesday night. This strong northerly flow\nwould produce a downslope effect for much of the Colorado\nPlains, and limit precipitation potential. In fact, this would\nbring very little precipitation to the Pueblo area compared to\nthe ECMWF (GFS only has 0.2 inches, the ECMWF nearly 3 inches,\nand NBM around 2.5 inches). Given the GFS being an outlier, have\nfollowed the ECMWF and NBM solutions during this period.\n\nA Flash Flood Watch has been issued for portions of the Plains\nwhere the highest confidence lies. Areas along the New Mexico\nborder are included along with the Southern Sangre de Cristo\nrange. DESI probabilities of 3+ inches are around 40-60 percent\nin this area. For areas north (along the Highway 50 corridor) we\nwill have to see how model guidance trends for any northward\nexpansion of the watch.\n\nSnow levels continue to look high; above 12 kft, where several\ninches may be possible Tuesday through Thursday.  \n\nTemperatures will be cooler during this period, with Plains\nmainly in the 50s and 60s, with the coolest days Wednesday and\nThursday.\n\nFriday through Monday...given the uncertainty in the nearer\nterm, there is a bit better agreement over the weekend into\nearly next week. The upper system will track east, with high\npressure building over the Great Basin. This will allow for\ndrying conditions and rebounding temperatures. Highs will warm\nback into the 70s over the weekend into Monday.",
